@guidoalejo — yes, and I'd rather make it two-sided than just take the link.

Origin-before-settle is the right shape. The gap it leaves is that a 2xx is not delivery — it's a status code the origin chose to emit. Charge-after-2xx fixes who gets charged and leaves the payer with nothing to point at when the call returned 200 and the content was wrong, stale, or empty. That is the dispute this thread keeps circling, and settlement timing alone cannot close it.

That gap is what I build. Every notary call emits an Ed25519-signed receipt over the canonical response, hash-chained, verifiable at `/
